#{[#edb/ref 17592186068553 "First, let's compute the step size $\\Delta x,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}=\\dfrac{2-0}{4}=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our situation. We are using a left Riemann sum, which means we draw the rectangles so that the left corner of each rectangle coincides with the curve.\n\n![](images/56/56427bec8108b822c4e1980725073cedc9fc79575d606014f72ea6ca9f8959c2.png)\n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A≈(16+9+4+1)\\cdot 0.5=15\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186068640 "The area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula for the left Riemann sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(-4)}}+{\\color{red}{f(-2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(5+3+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(8+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $\\mathcal A$ must be $28,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+2k & =28 \\\\ 2k & =12 \\\\ k & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186078289 "The sum takes integer values of $n$ from $2$ to $4.$ So, we compute the terms for $n=2,3,4$ and write the sum, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=2}^{4}(2n-3)&= \\overbrace{\\big(2(2)-3\\big)}^{\\large n\\,=\\,2}+\\overbrace{\\big(2(3)-3\\big)}^{\\large n\\,=\\,3}+\\overbrace{\\big(2(4)-3\\big)}^{\\large n\\,=\\,4} \\[-5pt]\n&=(4-3)+(6-3)+(8-3)\\[5pt]\n&=1+3+5\t\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186092003 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/1d/1dcbaa5d87db0d625e62399322ebd21723f1cb30269a8b49a3ad7d43e5f442c8.png)\n\nFirst, let's compute the step size $\\Delta{x}$, which is just the width of each rectangle. In our case, the endpoints of the interval are $a=0.5$ and $b=3.5$, and there are $n=3$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta{x} = \\dfrac{3.5-0.5}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the midpoints $x=1,2,3$, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A \\approx (\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(3)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}}) \\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the midpoints of each subinterval:\n\n| **Subinterval** | $x^\\ast$ | $f(x^\\ast)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $[0.5,1.5]$ | $1$ | $12$ |\n| $[1.5,2.5]$ | $2$ | $9$ |\n| $[2.5,3.5]$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx (12 + 9 + 4) \\cdot 1 = 25\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[0.5, 3.5]$, the \"extra\" area between the rectangles and the function is greater than the \"missing\" area, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area." #edb/ref 13194139533354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186105577 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(11+6+3+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186105648 "The area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula for the right Riemann sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(-2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+k+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $\\mathcal A$ must be $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n2k+10 & =12\\[5pt]\n      2k &= 12-10\\[5pt]\n        k &= \\dfrac{2}{2}\\[5pt]\n         k  &= 1.\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186105649 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(11+6+3+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." #edb/ref 13194139535876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186147173 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n4^2\\,+\\underbrace{(5-3)}_{\\large\\color{blue} 2}  =4^2\\,+ {\\color{blue} 2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{4^2}^{\\large\\color{red} 16}+2 = {\\color{red} 16}+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n16+2 = 18\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n4^2 +(5-3)=18.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186147174 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n28\\div \\underbrace{(7-5)}_{\\large\\color{blue} 2}{}^2   =28\\div {\\color{blue} 2}^2\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n28\\div\\overbrace{2^2}^{\\large\\color{red} 4} =28\\div {\\color{red} 4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **D**ivision:\n\n$$\n28\\div 4 =7\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n28\\div(7-5)^2=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186147175 "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $\\left(3+2^2\\right).$\n\nTo evaluate $\\left(3+2^2\\right),$ we should again apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n3\\,+\\overbrace{2^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 4} =3+ {\\color{blue} 4}\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n3 + 4 =  {\\color{red} 7}\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\underbrace{\\left(3+2^2\\right)}_{\\large\\color{red} 7}  =5\\times {\\color{red} 7}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that we have calculated the expression inside the **P**arentheses, we can finally move on to the **M**ultiplication:\n\n$$\n5 \\times 7 = 35\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5\\times\\left(3+2^2\\right)=35.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186151863 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1896, and April 6th, 1896. \n\n- There were $2$ months with $31$ days (January and March).\n\n- There were $0$ months with $30$ days.\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February) since it was a leap year.\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and April 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd \n= \\overbrace{31(2) + 30(0) + 29(1)}^{\\textrm{days to April 1st}} + 5.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31 \\equiv 3, \\qquad 30\\equiv 2, \\qquad 29\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =2(31)+0(30)+1(29)+5 \\\\  & ≡2(3)+0(2)+1(1)+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡6+0+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $5$ days after January 1st, 1896, namely Monday." #edb/ref 13194139533599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157066 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP \\quad \\Rightarrow \\overbrace{{\\color{blue}Q}}^{\\textrm{Always true}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always false. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is *not* always true. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is always true. $\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157067 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP \\quad \\Rightarrow \\overbrace{{\\color{blue}Q}}^{\\textrm{Always true}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, we proceed as follows:\n\nThe theorem can be written as the implication \n\n$$\nn\\gt 4\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nn^2 + \\dfrac{1}{n} > 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $n\\in\\mathbb{N},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+\\frac{1}{n} & >{n}^{2} \\\\  & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following fact:\n\nThe consequent $n^2 + \\dfrac{1}{n} > 1$ of our implication is always true.\n\nFinally, we can make the following conclusion:\n\nTherefore, the theorem is trivially true." #edb/ref 13194139533458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157068 "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overbrace{{\\color{blue}P}}^{\\textrm{Always false}} \\Rightarrow \\quad Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always true. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is *not* always false. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is always false. $\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171688 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7  x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 0.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.25}{42}} \\approx 0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] =  0.7 \\pm 0.3\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171696 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x = 21.3 + 10x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 10.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.05} = 2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing $S_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(4-5)^{2}+(4.5-5)^{2}+(5.5-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0.25+0.25+1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]  = 2.92 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.375}{2.5}} \\approx 2.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 10 \\pm 2.2\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171760 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} \\leq \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = 0.025\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96\n$$\n\nWe're given the estimate\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 84\\% = 0.84\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.06, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=0.84, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=1.96\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}); = ((1.96)^{2}0.84(1 - 0.84))/((0.06)^{2}); = 143.42\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that\n\n$$\nn=\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}144}\n$$\n\nor larger." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171761 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $E$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} \\leq E \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{E^2}\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\widehat{\\,p}$, and consequently, we do not know $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ either. However, to find a lower bound for $n$, we can simply replace the expression $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with its maximum value for $\\widehat{\\,p}\\in [0,1]$.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ea9a3d120e0cd3907308a00182d86c94f1b8e23c61ec146e6b49c2ace93ea8.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the maximum value of $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ occurs when\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand therefore, the maximum value of $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\\cdot \\left(1-\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac14\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2}{4E^2}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.576) = 0.005\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.005} = 2.576\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\nE=0.08, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=2.576\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge \\frac{z_{α/2}^{2}}{4E^{2}}; = ((2.576)^{2})/(4(0.08)^{2}); \\approx 259.21\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=260$ or larger." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171762 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nGiven a particular margin or error $\\varepsilon$, we can deduce an approximate sample size:\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} = \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nLet $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ be the margins of error of $I_1$ and $I_2$, respectively. Since we want $I_2$ to have the same length as $I_1$, then\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon_1 = \\varepsilon_2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{1} &= (z_{0.05}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε_{1}^{2}), \\\\\nn_{2} &= (z_{0.005}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε_{2}^{2}), \\\\\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{z_{0.005}^{2}}{z_{0.05}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nz_{0.05} = 1.645\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_{0.005}=2.576\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n_2}{n_1} = \\dfrac{2.576^2}{1.645^2} \\approx 2.5\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nn_2\\approx 2.5 n_1\n$$\n\nFinally, $n_2$ must be $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{larger}}$ than $n_1$ by a factor of approximately $2.5$." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171763 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} \\leq \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $98\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.98=0.02 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.01\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.326) = 0.01\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.01} = 2.326\n$$\n\nWe have the estimate $p \\in [0.6, 0.72]$. So, to find our lower bound for $n$, we replace $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with the maximum of the function\n\n$$\nf(p) = p(1-p)\n$$\n\nfor $p\\in[0.6, 0.72]$.\n\n![](images/31/31ff79710bc98bba503fd10accbb0118d596fc5fa4684e4c376f31f8183adcd6.png)\n\nSince $f$ decreases in the given interval, the maximum occurs at $p=0.6$. Therefore, we replace $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with\n\n$$\n0.6(1-0.6)=0.24\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.04, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=0.24, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=2.326\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}); = ((2.326)^{2}0.24)/((0.04)^{2}); = 811.54\n$$\n\nRounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=812$ or larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\nn_{\\textrm{min}} = 812\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171764 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, if the sample size $n$ is *sufficiently large*, then, according to the central limit theorem, the sample mean is approximately normally distributed.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $\\sigma$ is the population standard deviation, and\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n} \\leq \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\sigma^2}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $98\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.98=0.02 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.01\n$$\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that\n\n$$\nz_{0.01}=2.326{:}\n$$\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.010}$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.326}$ | $2.576$ |\n\nWe're given the population standard deviation $\\sigma=1.2$. Therefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.3, \\qquad \\sigma=1.2, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=2.326\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge \\frac{z_{α/2}^{2}σ^{2}}{ε^{2}}; = ((2.326)^{2}(1.2)^{2})/((0.3)^{2}); \\approx 86.56\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=87$ or larger. Notice that\n\n$$\n87\\geq 30\n$$\n\ntherefore, the sample is sufficiently large to apply the normal approximation." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171765 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population standard deviation $\\sigma$.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm \\overbrace{t_{n-1, \\,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt n}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $s$ is the sample standard deviation, and\n- $P(T > t_{n-1, \\,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nt_{n-1, \\,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt n} \\leq \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n \\geq \\dfrac{t_{n-1, \\,\\alpha/2}^2 \\, s^2}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nNote that the above lower bound can't be directly calculated because we need $n$ to determine $t_{n-1, \\,\\alpha/2}$, and $n$ is the value we are trying to find. However, as $n$ increases, the $t$-distribution approaches the standard normal distribution. Therefore, we can replace $t_{n-1, \\,\\alpha/2}$ by $z_{\\alpha/2}$, where\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z$ follows a standard normal distribution.\n\nNow, we are interested in finding a $98\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.98=0.02 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.01\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.326) = 0.01\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.01} = 2.326\n$$\n\nWe're given the sample standard deviation $s=0.5$. Therefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.05, \\qquad s=0.5, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=2.326\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge \\frac{z_{α/2}^{2}s^{2}}{ε^{2}}; = ((2.326)^{2}(0.5)^{2})/((0.05)^{2}); \\approx 541.03\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get $n=542$ or larger." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171766 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\overline{\\,x}$ is the sample mean,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $\\sigma$ is the population standard deviation, and\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n} \\leq \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\sigma^2}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nTo find an estimate for $\\sigma^2$, we can apply the empirical rule. According to this rule, $99.7\\%$ of the observations of a random normal variable $N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ fall in the interval $(\\mu-3\\sigma, \\mu+3\\sigma)$. Therefore, we can estimate $\\sigma$ by dividing the given range by\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3 = 6{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\sigma} =\\dfrac{12.7-(-5.3)}{6} = 3\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = 0.025\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.5, \\qquad \\widehat{\\sigma}=3, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=1.96\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge (z_{α/2}^{2}σ^(^)^{2})/(ε^{2}); = ((1.96)^{2}(3)^{2})/((0.5)^{2}); \\approx 138.30\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=139$ or larger." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171767 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\overline{\\,x}$ is the sample mean,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $\\sigma$ is the population standard deviation, and\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nLet $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ be the margins of error of $I_1$ and $I_2$, respectively. Since we want $I_2$ to be equal to $I_1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon_1 = \\varepsilon_2\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nz_{0.025} \\cdot \\dfrac{s_1}{\\sqrt{n_1}} = z_{0.01} \\cdot \\dfrac{s_2}{\\sqrt{n_2}}\n$$\n\nSolving for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}} &= \\frac{z_{0.01} \\cdot s_{2}}{z_{0.025} \\cdot s_{1}} \\\\\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}})^{2} &= (\\frac{z_{0.01} \\cdot s_{2}}{z_{0.025} \\cdot s_{1}})^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{z_{0.01}^{2} \\cdot s_{2}^{2}}{z_{0.025}^{2} \\cdot s_{1}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\ns_1 = 5, s_2 = 3, z_{0.01} = 2.326, z_{0.25} = 1.960\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{2.326^{2} \\cdot 3^{2}}{1.960^{2} \\cdot 5^{2}} \\approx 0.507\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nn_1\\approx 1.97 n_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n_2$ must be $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{smaller}}$ than $n_1$ by a factor of approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.97}$." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171768 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ for a small population can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $N$ is the population size satisfying the condition $n > 0.05\\cdot N$,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}} \\leq \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nDoing some algebra to isolate $n$, we get\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\dfrac{Nm}{N + m - 1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nis the minimum sample size needed when the population is large.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.576) = 0.005\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.005} = 2.576\n$$\n\nWe're given the estimate\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 10\\% = 0.1\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.02, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=0.1, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=2.576\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((2.576)^{2}0.1(1 - 0.1))/((0.02)^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 1493.05, \\\\\n&= n \\mid \\ge \\frac{Nm}{N + m - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{630 \\cdot 1493.05}{630 + 1493.05 - 1} \\\\\n&\\approx 443.26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=444$ or larger." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171769 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ for a small population can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $N$ is the population size satisfying the condition $n > 0.05\\cdot N$,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}} \\leq \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nDoing some algebra to isolate $n$, we get\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\dfrac{Nm}{N + m - 1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nis the minimum sample size needed when the population is large.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = 0.025\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\widehat{\\,p}$, and consequently, we do not know $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ either. However, to find a lower bound for $n$, we can simply replace the expression $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with its maximum value for $\\widehat{\\,p} \\in [0,1]$.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ea9a3d120e0cd3907308a00182d86c94f1b8e23c61ec146e6b49c2ace93ea8.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the maximum value of $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ occurs when\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand therefore, the maximum value of $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.06, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=\\dfrac 12, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=1.96\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((1.96)^{2}0.25)/((0.06)^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 266.78, \\\\\n&= n \\mid \\ge \\frac{Nm}{N + m - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{900 \\cdot 266.78}{900 + 266.78 - 1} \\\\\n&\\approx 205.96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=206$ or larger." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171770 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ for a small population can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $N$ is the population size satisfying the condition $n > 0.05\\cdot N$,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}} \\leq \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nDoing some algebra to isolate $n$ we get\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\dfrac{Nm}{N + m - 1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nis the minimum sample size needed when the population is large.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.576) = 0.005\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.005} = 2.576\n$$\n\nWe have the estimate $p \\in [0.1, 0.3]$. So, to found our lower bound for $n$, we replace $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with the maximum of the function\n\n$$\nf(p) = p(1-p)\n$$\n\nfor $p\\in[0.1, 0.3]$.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1e7ed2808d6361f7fb5a1c3531e5233ebf76a2554f8f63097c5c0a0d4fb210.png)\n\nSince $f$ increases in the given interval, the maximum occurs at $p=0.3$. Therefore, we replace $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with\n\n$$\n0.3(1-0.3)=0.21\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.03, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=0.3, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}= 2.576\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((2.576)^{2}0.3(1 - 0.3))/((0.03)^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 1548.35, \\\\\nn &= \\frac{Nm}{N + m - 1} \\\\\n&\\ge \\frac{8000 \\cdot 1548.35}{8000 + 1548.35 - 1} \\\\\n&= 1297.41\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=1298$ or larger." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186192387 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 1.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.90=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.25 \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{2.4^2}{42} \\bigg)} \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  1.6 \\pm 1.1\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186192388 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x= 21.3 + 10x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\beta_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=21.3 + 10x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 21.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.005} = 2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing $S_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(4-5)^{2}+(4.5-5)^{2}+(5.5-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0.25+0.25+1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 2.920 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.375\\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{5^2}{2.5} \\bigg)} \\approx 11.0\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  21.3 \\pm 11.0\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186192562 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\ncan be viewed as the sum of $9$ instances of the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} \n}^{\\large { {9} \\textrm{ times}}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nExamining the known fractions on the left-hand side of the given equation, from left to right, we have the following:\n\n- $2$ instances of $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ gives the fraction $\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}$\n- $4$ instances of $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ gives the fraction $\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}} + \\underbrace{\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}} +\n\t\\color{violet}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}  \n}^{\\large { {9} \\textrm{ times}}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nWe have $3$ instances of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1{10}\n$$\n\nremaining, and these give the fraction\n\n$$\n{\\color{violet}{\\dfrac3{10}}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10} }_{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}} + \n\t\\underbrace{\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10} }_{\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}} + \n\t\\underbrace{\\color{violet}\\dfrac{1}{10}+\\dfrac{1}{10}+ \\dfrac{1}{10}}_{\\color{violet}\\dfrac{3}{10}}\n}^{\\large { {9} \\textrm{ times}}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nThis gives the decomposition\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{10}} + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{10}}+ \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{violet}\\dfrac{3}{10}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing fraction is\n\n$$\n{\\color{violet}{\\dfrac3{10}}}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436]}
